b. LTV and DTI Calculation Logic Description
Loan-to-Value (LTV):

LTV measures the loan amount against the value of the collateral (home/property).

Formula:
LTV = (Loan Amount) / (Property Value)
Debt-to-Income (DTI):

DTI measures total monthly debt payments against monthly gross income.

Formula:
DTI = (Total Monthly Debt Payments) / (Gross Monthly Income)
Debt payments include loan payments, credit card minimums, etc., which are summed and divided by income.

4. Design Answers
a. Handling delayed credit bureau API data
If credit score data arrives later than application data, design a process to:

Ingest application data immediately into Bronze and Silver layers.
Implement incremental updates or joins in the Silver layer to enrich existing records when credit data arrives.
Use a watermark or status flag to track applications missing scores and backfill when data arrives.
Consider event-driven orchestration or retries to ensure credit scores are eventually matched.
b. Leveraging Snowflake’s Time Travel for auditing
Snowflake’s Time Travel allows querying historical data at a specific timestamp:

Use SELECT ... FROM table AT (TIMESTAMP => '2026-09-30 12:34:56') to view the applicant’s financial profile as it was at approval time.
This helps verify the exact data state at approval and supports compliance and audit requirements.
c. Scalability in end-of-quarter rush with 1000x submission spike
To handle spikes:

Use Snowflake’s multi-cluster warehouses to automatically scale compute resources based on workload.
Employ stream/batch processing to ingest data in near real-time without bottlenecks.
Decouple ingestion from transformations; load raw data quickly, then transform asynchronously.
Use orchestration tools to prioritize critical pipelines and increase concurrency limits temporarily.











